     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. Metformin 2,550 mg three times daily is 7,650 mg/day, three times the maximum labeled dose. I don't know of any therapeutic reason for that. It puts the patient at serious risk of lactic acidosis, and that risk doesn't depend on normal kidney and liver function.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- **850 mg PO TID with meals**, which is 2,550 mg/day, the usual maximum.\n- **1,000 mg PO BID with meals**, which is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **500 mg PO BID**, a standard starting dose.\n\nIf you do have an unusual clinical reason for the higher dose, such as a protocol or a research setting, it should go through direct pharmacy review and your institution's override process, not through me.\n\nTell me which of the options you'd like and I'll enter it."
